# APZHUB Step 7 — Identity system (locked)

This step locks the **authentication and identity boundary** for APZHUB.

**Principle:** APZHUB must **own the login experience** even when federation sits underneath. Users sign into **APZHUB**, not unrelated identity products.

## 7.1 Identity model (hybrid, separated concerns)

Clear separation:

- **A. Authentication** — how the user proves who they are  
- **B. Identity profile** — who the user is **in APZHUB**  
- **C. Federation** — how external IDPs connect  
- **D. Service trust** — how downstream apps trust the authenticated user  

Related, not the same.

## 7.2 Core identity rule

- **IDP** = auth engine (secure auth, standards compliance)  
- **APZHUB** = identity **experience** and **orchestration** (system-of-engagement)  

User sees APZHUB login; APZHUB manages routing, branding, session; IDP handles secure auth. **Right balance.**

## 7.3 Login experience

**One clean login page** only.

**Include:** APZHUB branding, email/username, password, sign in, optional “Sign in with Google” when enabled, forgot password, minimal support/help.

**Exclude:** marketing blocks, three-column layouts, long text, product promo, noisy illustrations.

Feel: **controlled business desktop product** entry point.

## 7.4 Login flow (default)

1. Open APZHUB  
2. Credentials on APZHUB login page  
3. APZHUB authenticates through IDP  
4. APZHUB establishes **its own** app session  
5. Route to **end-user workspace** or **admin home** by effective platform role  

## 7.5 Session model (two layers)

**Layer 1 — Identity session:** IDP-managed; auth and federation continuity.

**Layer 2 — APZHUB app session:** APZHUB-managed for UI routing, workspace assembly, access checks, launcher, profile/theme, linked account availability.

APZHUB must **not** depend on raw IDP state for all UI behavior.

## 7.6 User identity record in APZHUB

Application-facing profile (IDP does auth; APZHUB owns app profile), e.g.:

- Internal user ID, email, display name, status  
- Platform role, linked accounts  
- Theme, density preference  
- Last login, session flags  
- Service access assignments  

## 7.7 Google account linking

**Profile-level**, not forced login requirement.

**Purpose:** calendar, recent mail, Google launch convenience, richer productivity context later.

**Flow:** Profile → “Connect Google” → consent → secure token reference → widgets active.

**If not linked:** proper connect state; no broken panels; no fake content.

Google = **enrichment and convenience**, not the only identity model.

## 7.8 Federation model

**Phase 1:** local username/password through IDP-backed boundary; optional Google sign-in.

**Phase 2:** OIDC federation, SAML for enterprise, possible JIT user creation, possible domain-based routing later.

Design now for **local + social + enterprise** without changing the application model.

## 7.9 Standards support

- **OIDC** — modern apps, SSO, token identity flows, future internal federation  
- **SAML** — enterprise legacy, specific third-party  
- **JWT** — APZHUB FE/BE session claims, internal service trust, launch/session handoff  

**OIDC/SAML** = federation/auth standards. **JWT** = token format and trust. **Do not conflate** in design.

## 7.10 Role-aware post-login routing

After login determine: who, platform role, admin capability, target workspace.

**Defaults:** standard user → end-user home; admin/superadmin → admin home; mixed → **default assigned mode**, with authorized mode switch.

Keeps experiences **separated**.

## 7.11 Mode switching

Users with **both** admin and user capability: **no blended UI**.

- Log in once → default mode  
- **Explicit** mode switch (profile / launcher / header): **Workspace** | **Admin**  

Cleaner and safer than mixing controls.

## 7.12 Identity lifecycle states

Support clean lifecycle, e.g.: Invited, Active, Suspended, Disabled, Locked, Pending Federation, Pending Linked Account, Provisioning Pending.

Identity is not only “exists or not.”

## 7.13 Password and credential policy

**APZHUB-native login:** password reset, secure session expiry, optional MFA later, lockout/rate limits, no plaintext storage, no local shortcuts around IDP.

**Externally linked systems:** not user profile passwords — **Vault / service launch model** only.

**Separate:** APZHUB profile credentials vs external service credentials.

## 7.14 Identity admin functions

Admin can: create/invite, activate/deactivate, suspend/lock, reset sessions, view linked accounts, auth/federation state, trigger reprovisioning, audit trail.

**Must not** casually bypass security: no user passwords, no unsafe impersonation by default, no hidden auth shortcuts.

## 7.15 Audit requirements

Auditable: login success/failure, logout, password reset, account link/unlink, status change, session reset, federation events, mode switch where relevant.

Critical as central work entry point.

## 7.16 UI surfaces related to identity

One coherent system across:

- **Public login** — auth entry  
- **Profile** — theme, linked accounts, session/device (later)  
- **Admin / Users** — lifecycle and access  
- **Header** — profile, mode switch, linked status  
- **Launch system** — trust to downstream services  

## 7.17 What must be avoided

No: raw IDP UI to end users unless unavoidable; ugly federation-driven journeys; mandatory Google for core usage; merging APZHUB roles with service roles; admin identity controls spilling into end-user screens.

## Locked Step 7 statement

**APZHUB uses a hybrid identity architecture where a branded APZHUB login and application session sit in front of a standards-based IDP layer, supporting local authentication, optional Google linking, future OIDC/SAML federation, JWT-based internal trust, role-aware routing, and strict separation between identity, access, and downstream service permissions.**
